>> STATIC VS DYNAMIC
What you are thinking of (Conceptual/Product definition): 
A static prompt means the prompt is fixed and unchangeable for the user (every user gets the exact same thing). 
A dynamic prompt means the user has control and changes the input. 
By this definition, your Streamlit app is definitely dynamic.

What the mentor meant (Code-architecture definition): 
In that specific part of the video, he was contrasting raw, 
unstructured user strings (model.invoke(user_input)) against structured, 
reusable LangChain template objects (PromptTemplate). 
He loosely called the raw input approach a "static prompt workflow" simply 
because it lacks a built-in LangChain template structure with validation placeholders.

>> Why use prompt template and not f string
A PromptTemplate in LangChain is a structured way to create prompts dynamically by inserting variables into a predefined template. 
Instead of hardcoding prompts, PromptTemplate allows you to define placeholders that can be filled in at runtime with different inputs.
This makes it reusable, flexible, and easy to manage, especially when working with dynamic user inputs or automated workflows.
Why use PromptTemplate over f-strings?
1. Default validation
2. Reusable
3. LangChain ecosystem